// Detailed notes: concept id (or "term:<Term>") -> { body, example, tip }.
// Modules: Cloud Computing Foundations, Cloud Storage & Data, Cloud Architecture & Scalability,
// Cloud Data & Analytics, Cloud Monitoring & Observability, Cloud Governance & Compliance,
// Monitoring & Observability (advanced).
const n = (body, example, tip) => ({ body, example, tip });

export default {
  // ---------- Shared terms ----------
  'term:Data Lake': n(
    "A data lake is a central repository that stores large volumes of raw data in its native format (structured tables, semi-structured JSON and logs, and unstructured files such as images) at low cost, usually on object storage such as Amazon S3, Azure Data Lake Storage, or Google Cloud Storage. Unlike a warehouse, it applies schema on read: structure is imposed when the data is queried, not when it is loaded.\n\nThat flexibility suits machine learning, log analytics, IoT data, and questions you have not thought of yet. Query engines such as Athena, Spark, Presto/Trino, and BigQuery external tables read directly from the lake, especially efficiently when data is stored in columnar formats (Parquet, ORC) and partitioned. Without governance (a data catalogue, ownership, quality checks, access control, and lifecycle policies), a lake degrades into a 'data swamp' that nobody trusts.",
    "A retailer lands clickstream JSON, point-of-sale CSVs, and product images in S3, then uses Spark for recommendation models and Athena for ad-hoc analysis.",
    "Classify and control access to sensitive data in the lake from day one. Lakes often become the largest store of PII in the company."
  ),
  'term:Distributed Tracing': n(
    "Distributed tracing follows a single request as it travels through many services. The first service creates a trace ID, and each unit of work (an HTTP call, database query, or queue message) records a span with its start time, duration, attributes, and parent span. Context propagation passes the trace ID between services in headers (the W3C traceparent standard), so the backend can reassemble the complete request tree.\n\nTraces show exactly where latency accumulates and where errors originate, which is nearly impossible to reconstruct from logs alone in microservice architectures. Tools include OpenTelemetry for instrumentation plus backends such as Jaeger, Zipkin, Tempo, AWS X-Ray, and commercial APM products. Because tracing every request is expensive, systems use sampling: head-based (decide at the start) or tail-based (keep traces that were slow or failed).",
    "A checkout request takes 4 seconds; its trace shows 3.5 seconds spent in one call from the pricing service to a third-party tax API.",
    "Add trace IDs to your logs so you can jump from a slow trace straight to the relevant log lines."
  ),

  // ---------- Cloud Computing Foundations ----------
  'cf-1': n(
    "Cloud computing is the on-demand delivery of computing resources (servers, storage, databases, networking, software, analytics, and AI) over the internet, with pay-as-you-go pricing. Instead of buying and running hardware in your own data centre, you rent capacity from a provider such as AWS, Microsoft Azure, or Google Cloud, and can provision it in minutes through a console or API.\n\nNIST defines five essential characteristics: on-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling (multi-tenancy), rapid elasticity, and measured service (pay for what you use). Benefits include speed, global reach, elasticity, and trading capital expense for operating expense. The trade-offs are ongoing costs that must be managed, dependence on the provider, and new security responsibilities for configuration and identity.",
    "A startup launches its app worldwide without buying a single server, scaling from 10 to 10,000 users in a month by adding cloud resources as needed.",
    "Remember NIST's five characteristics; they are a common exam definition question."
  ),
  'cf-2': n(
    "Infrastructure as a Service provides virtualised computing building blocks: virtual machines, block and object storage, virtual networks, load balancers, and IP addresses, which you configure much like physical hardware. Examples include Amazon EC2, Azure Virtual Machines, and Google Compute Engine.\n\nIaaS gives the most flexibility and control among the service models: you choose the operating system, install any software, and configure networking and security in detail. It also brings the most responsibility. You must patch and harden the OS, manage middleware and applications, configure firewalls, back up data, and control access. The provider manages the physical data centre, hardware, and virtualisation layer. IaaS suits lift-and-shift migrations, custom software stacks, and workloads needing specific OS control.",
    "A company migrates its legacy ERP servers to EC2 instances unchanged, keeping full control of the Windows Server OS and the application.",
    "In IaaS, OS patching is your job. Unpatched cloud VMs are breached just like on-premises servers."
  ),
  'cf-3': n(
    "Platform as a Service provides a managed environment for building, deploying, and running applications without managing the underlying servers or operating systems. You supply the code (and configuration); the provider handles the OS, runtime, middleware, patching, scaling, and often load balancing and monitoring. Examples include Heroku, AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Azure App Service, Google App Engine, and managed databases (RDS, Cloud SQL), which are often counted as PaaS.\n\nPaaS speeds development and reduces operational toil, but it offers less control over the environment, may constrain supported languages and versions, and can create lock-in to provider-specific features. Customers remain responsible for application code security, configuration, identity and access, and data.",
    "A development team deploys a Python web app to Azure App Service with `git push`, while Microsoft patches the OS and scales instances automatically.",
    "PaaS moves OS responsibility to the provider, but your code, configuration, secrets, and access control are still yours."
  ),
  'cf-4': n(
    "Software as a Service delivers complete applications over the internet, usually through a browser or mobile app, on a subscription basis. The provider runs everything: infrastructure, operating system, application, updates, and availability. Examples include Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, Salesforce, Slack, Zoom, and Dropbox.\n\nFor users, SaaS means no installation or maintenance and access from anywhere. The customer still owns critical security responsibilities: configuring the service securely, managing who has access (ideally through SSO with MFA), protecting and classifying data, controlling sharing settings, and monitoring activity. SaaS sprawl and shadow IT (employees signing up for unapproved apps) are common governance challenges.",
    "A company uses Microsoft 365 for email and documents; Microsoft runs the servers, but the company must configure MFA, sharing policies, and data retention itself.",
    "Most SaaS data breaches are customer-side misconfigurations, such as publicly shared links or no MFA, not provider hacks."
  ),
  'cf-5': n(
    "Public cloud infrastructure is owned and operated by a third-party provider and shared by many customers (tenants) over the internet. Each customer's resources are logically isolated through virtualisation, networking, and identity controls, while the physical hardware is pooled.\n\nAdvantages include no upfront hardware cost, massive scale, global regions, rapid innovation, and pay-per-use pricing. Considerations include ongoing spend management, data residency, dependence on the provider's availability, and correctly configuring your own security. The major providers are AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud, followed by Oracle, IBM, and Alibaba Cloud. Dedicated options such as dedicated hosts give single-tenant hardware inside the public cloud when licensing or compliance requires it.",
    "An e-commerce company runs entirely on AWS, scaling up for holiday traffic and paying only for the extra capacity during those weeks.",
    "Multi-tenancy is safe when isolation holds. Provider isolation is strong, but your configuration determines your exposure."
  ),
  'cf-6': n(
    "A private cloud provides cloud capabilities (self-service, automation, and elastic pooled resources) to a single organisation. It can run on-premises in the organisation's own data centre (for example with VMware Cloud Foundation, OpenStack, or Nutanix) or be hosted by a provider on dedicated hardware.\n\nPrivate clouds offer greater control over hardware, data location, security configuration, and customisation, which appeals to regulated industries, organisations with strict data sovereignty needs, and steady, predictable workloads. The trade-offs are higher capital costs, the organisation's own responsibility for capacity, maintenance, and staffing, and less elasticity: you can only scale to the hardware you own.",
    "A bank runs its core transaction systems on an on-premises private cloud for regulatory control, while its marketing site runs in the public cloud.",
    "A virtualised data centre is not automatically a private cloud; it needs self-service and automation too."
  ),
  'cf-7': n(
    "Hybrid cloud combines public cloud with private cloud or on-premises infrastructure, connected so workloads and data can move between them or work together. Connectivity typically uses VPNs or dedicated links (Direct Connect, ExpressRoute), with consistent identity, networking, security policy, and management across environments.\n\nOrganisations choose hybrid to keep sensitive or latency-critical systems on-premises while using the public cloud for elasticity (cloud bursting), new applications, analytics, or disaster recovery; to migrate gradually; or to meet data residency rules. Tools such as Azure Arc, AWS Outposts, Google Anthos, and Kubernetes help manage hybrid estates. Multi-cloud, using several public clouds, is related but different.",
    "A hospital keeps patient records on-premises for compliance, but runs its analytics and patient-portal website in Azure, linked by ExpressRoute.",
    "Hybrid multiplies the attack surface. Apply consistent identity, logging, and security policies across every environment."
  ),
  'cf-8': n(
    "Capital expenditure (CapEx) is spending on long-lived assets, such as servers, storage arrays, and data-centre build-outs, paid up front and depreciated over several years. It requires forecasting capacity years ahead, often leading to over-provisioning (idle waste) or under-provisioning (outages and slowdowns).\n\nOperational expenditure (OpEx) is ongoing spending on services consumed, such as cloud usage billed monthly. Cloud shifts IT spending from CapEx to OpEx: no large upfront purchases, costs that track actual usage, and faster experimentation. But OpEx can grow unexpectedly without cost management, and steady long-term workloads can sometimes be cheaper on owned hardware or with committed-use discounts (Reserved Instances, Savings Plans).",
    "Instead of spending $500,000 up front on servers for a new product, a company runs it in the cloud for $8,000 a month and scales spending as revenue grows.",
    "Cloud is not automatically cheaper. It is more flexible. Cost optimisation is a continuous discipline."
  ),
  'cf-9': n(
    "Elasticity is the ability to automatically add resources when demand rises and remove them when it falls, closely matching capacity to load in near real time. Scalability is the broader ability of a system to handle growth, either vertically (a bigger instance) or horizontally (more instances). Elasticity is scalability that happens automatically and in both directions.\n\nCloud services achieve elasticity through auto-scaling groups, serverless functions that scale per request (including down to zero), managed databases with automatic capacity, and container orchestration. The benefit is paying only for what is used while handling spikes gracefully. Applications must be designed for it: stateless servers, externalised session state, and fast startup times.",
    "A ticketing site scales from 4 to 200 instances in minutes when concert tickets go on sale, then back down to 4 an hour later.",
    "Scale out (more instances) is usually preferred to scale up (bigger instances) for elasticity and resilience."
  ),
  'cf-10': n(
    "A Region is a separate geographic area (for example AWS us-east-1 in Northern Virginia, or Azure West Europe) containing multiple Availability Zones. Each AZ is one or more discrete data centres with independent power, cooling, and networking, located far enough apart to avoid shared disasters but connected by low-latency private links.\n\nDeploying across multiple AZs protects against the failure of a single data centre, which is the standard for high availability. Deploying across multiple Regions protects against a whole-region outage and serves users with lower latency worldwide, but adds cost and data-replication complexity. Region choice also affects data residency and compliance, service availability, and price. Edge locations, local zones, and wavelength zones push some services even closer to users.",
    "A web app runs in three AZs within eu-west-1, so an entire data centre can fail with no customer impact, and data stays in the EU for GDPR.",
    "Multi-AZ = high availability; multi-region = disaster recovery and global latency. Know the difference."
  ),

  // ---------- Cloud Storage & Data ----------
  'cs-1': n(
    "Object storage stores data as objects, each consisting of the data itself, metadata, and a unique key, inside flat containers called buckets. It is accessed over HTTP(S) APIs (PUT, GET, DELETE) rather than mounted as a file system. Services include Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage, and Google Cloud Storage, with S3-compatible options such as MinIO and Wasabi.\n\nObject storage scales virtually without limit, is extremely durable (S3 is designed for 99.999999999%, 'eleven nines', of durability by replicating across multiple facilities), and is cheap per gigabyte, with storage classes for different access patterns. It is ideal for backups, logs, media, data lakes, and static website hosting. It is not suited to databases or anything needing frequent small in-place updates. Security relies on bucket policies, IAM, blocking public access, encryption, and versioning or object lock.",
    "A photo-sharing app stores billions of user images in S3 and serves them through a CDN, while metadata lives in a database.",
    "Misconfigured public buckets are a top cause of cloud data leaks. Enable 'Block Public Access' at the account level."
  ),
  'cs-2': n(
    "Block storage provides raw storage volumes that attach to a virtual machine and appear to its operating system as a local disk, which it formats with a file system. Examples include Amazon EBS, Azure Managed Disks, and Google Persistent Disk. Data is stored in fixed-size blocks, giving low latency and high IOPS, ideal for boot volumes, databases, and transactional applications.\n\nVolumes persist independently of the instance: they can be detached and reattached, resized, snapshotted, and encrypted. They live in a single Availability Zone and generally attach to one instance at a time (some multi-attach options exist for clustered applications). Performance tiers range from cost-effective general-purpose SSDs to provisioned-IOPS SSDs for demanding databases and throughput-optimised HDDs for large sequential workloads.",
    "A PostgreSQL server on EC2 stores its data on a provisioned-IOPS EBS volume, with nightly snapshots copied to another region for disaster recovery.",
    "Block volumes are zonal. To survive an AZ failure, replicate the data or restore from snapshots in another AZ."
  ),
  'cs-3': n(
    "Cloud file storage provides a fully managed shared file system that many servers can mount at the same time using standard protocols: NFS (Linux) or SMB (Windows). Examples include Amazon EFS and FSx, Azure Files, and Google Filestore. It offers a familiar hierarchical directory structure with file locking and permissions.\n\nIt suits workloads that need shared access to the same files: content management systems, shared home directories, media processing, legacy lift-and-shift applications expecting a network share, and container volumes shared across pods. Managed file services scale capacity automatically and can span AZs. They cost more per gigabyte than object storage and may have higher latency than local block storage, so choose based on access pattern.",
    "Ten web servers behind a load balancer all mount the same EFS file system, so uploaded images are instantly visible to every server.",
    "Shared storage for many servers = file storage. One server with high IOPS = block storage. Massive cheap unstructured data = object storage."
  ),
  'cs-3b': n(
    "Network Attached Storage (NAS) is a dedicated file server or appliance that shares files over a standard network using file-level protocols (SMB/CIFS for Windows, NFS for Linux and Unix). Clients see folders and files; the NAS manages the file system. NAS is simple to deploy, relatively inexpensive, works with virtually any OS, and is ideal for shared documents, home directories, and backups, but the file-protocol layer and network add overhead.\n\nA Storage Area Network (SAN) is a dedicated high-speed network that presents raw block storage (LUNs) to servers, which format and manage the file systems themselves, as if the storage were a local disk. SANs use Fibre Channel (with HBAs and FC switches) or iSCSI over Ethernet, and deliver very high performance, low latency, and features such as snapshots and replication. They are more complex and expensive, and suit databases, virtualisation clusters, and high-performance applications.",
    "An office uses a Synology NAS for shared department folders, while its VMware cluster runs on a Fibre Channel SAN for performance and live migration.",
    "NAS = file-level access over the network (SMB/NFS). SAN = block-level access (Fibre Channel/iSCSI). A classic exam pairing."
  ),
  'cs-3c': n(
    "RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks) combines multiple drives into one logical volume for performance, redundancy, or both. RAID 0 (striping) splits data across disks for speed and full capacity but has no redundancy: one failed disk loses everything. RAID 1 (mirroring) writes identical copies to two disks and survives one failure, at half the capacity. RAID 5 stripes data with distributed parity across three or more disks and survives one disk failure. RAID 6 uses double parity with four or more disks and survives two failures.\n\nRAID 10 (1+0) stripes across mirrored pairs, giving high performance and redundancy, and is favoured for databases, at 50% capacity. Hardware RAID uses a dedicated controller, often with battery-backed cache; software RAID (mdadm, Storage Spaces, ZFS) uses the host CPU and is more flexible. Rebuilding large arrays takes a long time, and a second failure during a rebuild is a real risk, so RAID 6 or 10 is preferred for big disks.",
    "A file server with six 8 TB disks in RAID 6 loses one disk, keeps running, and rebuilds onto a hot spare while staying protected against another failure.",
    "RAID is not a backup. It does not protect against deletion, ransomware, corruption, or site loss. Keep separate backups."
  ),
  'cs-4': n(
    "A managed database service runs a database engine for you: provisioning, OS and engine patching, automated backups with point-in-time restore, monitoring, replication, and automatic failover. Examples include Amazon RDS and Aurora, Azure SQL Database, Google Cloud SQL and AlloyDB, and managed NoSQL such as DynamoDB, Cosmos DB, and MongoDB Atlas.\n\nYou keep responsibility for schema design, query performance, access control, network exposure, encryption settings, and choosing the right size and tier. In return you avoid most DBA operational toil and get high availability (for example Multi-AZ deployments with synchronous standby replicas) with a few settings. The trade-offs are less control over the engine (limited superuser access and extensions) and higher cost than self-managed databases on VMs.",
    "A team moves from a self-managed MySQL VM to RDS Multi-AZ; when the primary's AZ has an outage, RDS fails over to the standby in about a minute.",
    "Keep managed databases in private subnets, require TLS, and use IAM or secret rotation instead of shared static passwords."
  ),
  'cs-5': n(
    "Cloud NoSQL databases (Amazon DynamoDB, Azure Cosmos DB, Google Firestore and Bigtable, MongoDB Atlas) store data in non-relational models: key-value, document, wide-column, or graph. They typically have flexible schemas and scale horizontally across many nodes, delivering consistent single-digit-millisecond latency at very high throughput.\n\nThey excel at predictable access patterns at scale: user profiles and sessions, shopping carts, product catalogues, IoT telemetry, gaming leaderboards, and event data. Design starts from the queries (access patterns) rather than normalised tables, and partition keys must spread load evenly to avoid hot partitions. Many offer tunable consistency, global multi-region replication, serverless on-demand capacity, time-to-live expiry, and change streams.",
    "A mobile game stores player profiles and leaderboards in DynamoDB global tables, serving players on three continents with low latency.",
    "Choose a high-cardinality partition key (such as userId). A low-cardinality key (such as country) creates hot partitions and throttling."
  ),
  'cs-7': n(
    "A snapshot is a point-in-time copy of a storage volume, file system, or database. In the cloud, the first snapshot copies all data, and later snapshots are incremental, storing only blocks changed since the previous one, which makes frequent snapshots efficient. Snapshots are stored durably (for example in S3 behind the scenes for EBS).\n\nThey are used for backups, restoring after mistakes, creating new volumes and cloning environments, and migrating data across AZs or regions (by copying snapshots). For consistency, databases should be quiesced or use application-consistent snapshot tools. Automate snapshot schedules and retention (AWS Data Lifecycle Manager, AWS Backup), copy critical snapshots to another region or account, and protect them from deletion, because ransomware operators target backups.",
    "Before a risky upgrade, an admin snapshots the server's volumes; when the upgrade fails, a new volume from the snapshot restores service in minutes.",
    "Copy backups to a separate account with restricted access (or vault lock) so a compromised production account cannot delete them."
  ),
  'cs-8': n(
    "Storage tiers price storage according to how often data is accessed. Hot tiers (such as S3 Standard) offer instant, frequent access at the highest storage price. Cool or infrequent-access tiers (S3 Standard-IA, Azure Cool) cost less to store but charge per retrieval and may have minimum storage durations. Archive tiers (S3 Glacier and Glacier Deep Archive, Azure Archive) are the cheapest to store, but retrieval takes minutes to hours and costs more.\n\nLifecycle policies automate transitions, for example moving logs to infrequent access after 30 days, to archive after 90, and deleting after 7 years, which dramatically cuts costs for aging data. Intelligent tiering services move objects automatically based on observed access. Consider retrieval time requirements, retrieval fees, and minimum durations when choosing a tier.",
    "A company keeps 30 days of logs in hot storage for troubleshooting, then archives them to Glacier Deep Archive for compliance, cutting storage costs by over 90%.",
    "Archive tiers are cheap to store but slow and costly to restore. Make sure restore times meet your RTO."
  ),
  'cs-9': n(
    "Data replication maintains copies of data in multiple locations so it survives failures and can be served closer to users. Within a region, managed services replicate across Availability Zones (for example Multi-AZ databases and S3's multi-facility storage), protecting against a data-centre outage. Cross-region replication (S3 CRR, database read replicas and global tables, geo-redundant storage) protects against a regional disaster and supports global low-latency reads.\n\nSynchronous replication confirms writes on replicas before acknowledging them: no data loss on failover, but higher write latency. Asynchronous replication acknowledges immediately and replicates afterwards: faster, but recent writes can be lost on failover (non-zero RPO). Replication multiplies storage and transfer costs, and it faithfully replicates mistakes and ransomware encryption too, so it complements rather than replaces backups.",
    "An S3 bucket in us-east-1 replicates to eu-west-1, so the data survives even if an entire region becomes unavailable.",
    "Replication protects availability, not against deletion or corruption. You still need versioned, point-in-time backups."
  ),
  'cs-10': n(
    "Ephemeral (instance store) storage consists of disks physically attached to the host machine that runs a VM. It is often very fast NVMe SSD with high IOPS and no network latency, and it is usually included in the instance price. But data on it exists only as long as the instance runs on that host: stopping, terminating, or hibernating the instance, or a host failure, erases it.\n\nUse ephemeral storage only for temporary data that can be recreated: caches, buffers, scratch space for processing, swap, or replicated data in distributed systems that keep copies on other nodes (such as some Cassandra or Kafka deployments). Container file systems and many serverless /tmp directories are ephemeral too. Never keep the only copy of important data there.",
    "A video transcoding job writes intermediate files to fast instance-store NVMe, then uploads the finished video to S3 before the instance is terminated.",
    "Stopping an instance with instance-store volumes wipes them. Persistent data belongs on block, file, or object storage."
  ),

  // ---------- Cloud Architecture & Scalability ----------
  'ca-1': n(
    "Microservices architecture splits an application into small, independently deployable services, each owning one business capability (such as orders, payments, or inventory) and its own data store, and communicating over APIs or events. Teams can develop, deploy, and scale their services independently, choose appropriate technologies, and limit the blast radius of failures.\n\nThe costs are real: distributed-systems complexity (network latency and failures, retries, timeouts), data consistency across services (sagas instead of single transactions), harder testing and debugging (requiring distributed tracing and centralised logging), service discovery, versioned APIs, and heavier operational overhead. Many organisations do well starting with a well-structured modular monolith and splitting out services only when team size or scaling needs justify it.",
    "An e-commerce company splits checkout into its own service so it can scale independently during sales and deploy fixes without redeploying the product catalogue.",
    "Microservices trade code complexity for operational complexity. Invest in observability, CI/CD, and automation first."
  ),
  'ca-2': n(
    "Serverless computing lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. With Functions as a Service (AWS Lambda, Azure Functions, Google Cloud Functions), you upload a function and the provider runs it in response to events such as HTTP requests, file uploads, queue messages, and schedules, scaling automatically from zero to thousands of concurrent executions. You pay per invocation and per millisecond of execution, and nothing when idle.\n\nServerless also includes managed services such as API gateways, queues, and serverless databases. It suits event-driven, bursty, and glue workloads. Limitations include cold-start latency, maximum execution times (for example 15 minutes for Lambda), statelessness, per-request costs that can exceed containers at sustained high volume, and provider lock-in. Security focuses on least-privilege function roles, input validation, and dependency scanning.",
    "When a user uploads a photo to S3, a Lambda function automatically creates thumbnails. The company pays only for the milliseconds each resize takes.",
    "Give each function its own minimal IAM role. One over-privileged function can expose the whole account."
  ),
  'ca-3': n(
    "Auto scaling automatically adjusts the number of compute resources (VM instances, containers, and in some services database capacity) to match demand. Policies define minimum, maximum, and desired capacity. Target-tracking policies keep a metric near a target (for example average CPU at 60% or requests per target at 1,000); step policies react to alarm thresholds; scheduled scaling handles predictable patterns; and predictive scaling uses historical trends.\n\nScaling out adds capacity during spikes; scaling in removes it to save money when load falls. Auto scaling works with load balancers (new instances register automatically after passing health checks) and replaces unhealthy instances, which provides self-healing. Applications must be stateless and start quickly, and scale-in should drain connections gracefully. Cooldown periods prevent flapping.",
    "An auto-scaling group keeps CPU near 50%, running 3 instances overnight and 30 at lunchtime peak, and it automatically replaces an instance whose health check fails.",
    "Set a sensible maximum and a budget alert. A bug or attack can otherwise scale your bill without limit."
  ),
  'ca-4': n(
    "High availability means designing a system to keep operating through component failures with minimal downtime. The core principle is eliminating single points of failure through redundancy: multiple instances behind load balancers, spread across Availability Zones, replicated databases with automatic failover, redundant network paths, and health checks that detect failures and shift traffic automatically.\n\nAvailability is expressed in 'nines': 99.9% allows about 8.8 hours of downtime a year (about 43 minutes a month); 99.99% about 52.6 minutes a year; 99.999% about 5.3 minutes a year. Each extra nine costs significantly more. A system's overall availability is limited by its dependencies: chaining components multiplies their availabilities, so redundancy is needed at every critical layer.",
    "A web tier runs in three AZs; when one AZ fails, the load balancer routes all traffic to the remaining two, and customers notice nothing.",
    "Compound availability: two 99.9% dependencies in series give only about 99.8%. Remove single points of failure everywhere."
  ),
  'ca-5': n(
    "The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) is the maximum acceptable time to restore a service after a disruption: how quickly you must be back up. The Recovery Point Objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable data loss, measured in time: how far back your last good copy can be. They are business decisions, set through a business impact analysis, and they drive technical design and cost.\n\nAn RTO of 4 hours and an RPO of 24 hours might be met with nightly backups and a documented restore. An RTO of minutes and an RPO near zero needs synchronous replication and a hot standby or active-active multi-region design. Disaster-recovery strategies range from backup and restore, through pilot light and warm standby, to multi-site active-active, from cheapest and slowest to most expensive and fastest. Test restores regularly to prove the objectives can actually be met.",
    "An online store sets RTO 1 hour and RPO 5 minutes for checkout, so it runs a warm standby in a second region with continuous database replication.",
    "RTO = time to recover (downtime); RPO = data loss window. Also know MTD (maximum tolerable downtime); RTO must be shorter than MTD."
  ),
  'ca-6': n(
    "A multi-region architecture deploys an application across two or more geographically separate cloud regions. Active-passive designs keep a standby region ready (warm or pilot light) to take over if the primary fails. Active-active designs serve traffic from all regions at once, routing users to the nearest healthy region for low latency.\n\nMulti-region provides the highest resilience, surviving the loss of an entire region, and improves global performance, but it is the most complex and expensive option. Challenges include cross-region data replication and consistency (conflict resolution for multi-writer databases), global traffic routing (DNS latency or failover routing, global load balancers, anycast), duplicated infrastructure costs, and keeping configurations identical, which is best done with infrastructure as code and automated failover testing.",
    "A payments company runs active-active in us-east-1 and us-west-2 with DynamoDB global tables; a regional outage shifts traffic to the other region automatically.",
    "Most applications meet their goals with multi-AZ. Go multi-region only when the business impact of a region outage justifies the cost."
  ),
  'ca-8': n(
    "CI/CD automates the path from code change to production. Continuous Integration means developers merge small changes frequently into a shared branch, and every change triggers an automated build plus unit, integration, and security tests, so integration problems are caught within minutes. Continuous Delivery keeps the code always in a releasable state, with automated deployment through environments and a manual approval for production. Continuous Deployment removes the manual gate, so every change that passes the pipeline goes to production automatically.\n\nPipelines (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins, Azure DevOps, AWS CodePipeline) typically run: build, test, scan (SAST, dependency, container image, and IaC scans), package an immutable artifact, deploy to staging, run smoke tests, deploy to production using a safe strategy (rolling, blue/green, or canary), and monitor with automated rollback. Small, frequent releases lower risk.",
    "A developer merges a fix at 10:00; tests, security scans, and a canary deployment run automatically, and it reaches all users by 10:25 with no manual steps.",
    "Secure the pipeline itself. It holds production credentials, so a compromised CI system is a supply-chain attack path."
  ),
  'ca-9': n(
    "In an event-driven architecture, services communicate by producing and consuming events (records that something happened, such as 'OrderPlaced') through a message broker or event bus instead of calling each other directly. Examples include Amazon EventBridge, SNS and SQS, Apache Kafka, Azure Event Grid and Service Bus, and Google Pub/Sub.\n\nProducers do not need to know who consumes their events, so services are loosely coupled and can be added, scaled, or fail independently, and queues absorb traffic spikes and buffer during outages. The trade-offs include eventual consistency, harder end-to-end tracing and debugging, the need for idempotent consumers (events may be delivered more than once), ordering challenges, dead-letter queues for poison messages, and careful schema versioning.",
    "When an order is placed, one event triggers the inventory, email, analytics, and fraud services independently. Adding a loyalty-points service needs no change to the order service.",
    "Make consumers idempotent. At-least-once delivery means the same event can arrive twice."
  ),
  'ca-10': n(
    "FinOps is the practice of bringing financial accountability to variable cloud spending through collaboration between engineering, finance, and business teams. The FinOps Foundation describes a cycle of Inform (visibility and allocation: tagging, showback and chargeback, dashboards), Optimize (right-sizing, removing idle resources, choosing pricing models such as Savings Plans, Reserved Instances, and Spot, and storage lifecycle policies), and Operate (budgets, policies, forecasting, and continuous improvement).\n\nThe goal is not simply spending less but maximising business value per dollar: understanding unit economics such as cost per customer or per transaction. Common quick wins include shutting down non-production resources out of hours, deleting unattached volumes and old snapshots, right-sizing over-provisioned instances, and using committed-use discounts for steady workloads.",
    "Tagging reveals that 30% of spend comes from forgotten test environments; scheduling them to shut down nights and weekends saves $40,000 a month.",
    "You cannot optimise what you cannot attribute. Enforce cost-allocation tags from day one."
  ),

  // ---------- Cloud Data & Analytics ----------
  'cda-1': n(
    "A data warehouse is a central store of structured, cleaned, and integrated data optimised for analytics and business intelligence. Data from operational systems is loaded through ETL or ELT pipelines and modelled, commonly as star schemas with fact tables (measurements such as sales) and dimension tables (context such as customer, product, and date), so analysts can run fast SQL queries across large histories.\n\nCloud warehouses such as Snowflake, Amazon Redshift, Google BigQuery, and Azure Synapse separate storage from compute, use columnar storage and massively parallel processing, and scale on demand. They apply schema on write: data must fit the model before loading, which ensures consistency and trusted reporting. Warehouses are best when the questions are known and the data is structured.",
    "A retailer's warehouse combines sales, inventory, and marketing data so the finance team can report daily revenue by store, product, and campaign in seconds.",
    "Warehouses concentrate sensitive business data. Apply role-based access, column masking, and audit logging."
  ),
  'cda-3': n(
    "A lakehouse combines the low-cost, flexible storage of a data lake with the reliability and performance features of a warehouse. Open table formats such as Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, and Apache Hudi sit on top of files in object storage and add ACID transactions, schema enforcement and evolution, time travel (querying older versions), efficient updates and deletes, and metadata for fast queries.\n\nThis lets one copy of data serve both BI and SQL analytics and data science and ML, avoiding duplicate pipelines between separate lakes and warehouses. Platforms include Databricks, Snowflake with Iceberg tables, AWS (Athena, EMR, and Glue with Iceberg), and Microsoft Fabric. Governance catalogues such as Unity Catalog and AWS Lake Formation provide central access control and lineage.",
    "A bank stores transaction data once in Iceberg tables on S3; analysts query it with SQL for reports while data scientists train fraud models on the same tables.",
    "Time travel and ACID deletes make lakehouses practical for GDPR 'right to erasure' requests on large datasets."
  ),
  'cda-4': n(
    "ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) extracts data from sources, transforms it (cleaning, joining, aggregating, and conforming to the target schema) on a separate processing engine, and then loads the finished result into the warehouse. It suited older warehouses with expensive, limited compute, and it is still useful when sensitive data must be masked or filtered before it lands.\n\nELT (Extract, Load, Transform) loads raw data into the cloud warehouse or lakehouse first, then transforms it there using the platform's scalable compute, typically with SQL-based tools such as dbt. It is the modern cloud default: ingestion is faster and simpler, raw data is preserved for reprocessing when requirements change, and transformations are version-controlled and tested like code. Tools such as Fivetran and Airbyte handle extract and load.",
    "A company loads raw Salesforce and Stripe data into Snowflake nightly with Fivetran, then dbt models transform it into tested revenue and churn tables.",
    "With ELT, raw sensitive data lands first. Apply access controls and masking to the raw layer too."
  ),
  'cda-5': n(
    "Batch processing collects data over a period and processes it all at once on a schedule, such as hourly, nightly, or monthly. It is simpler, cheaper, easier to reprocess, and well suited to historical reporting, billing, payroll, and ML training. Its drawback is latency: results are only as fresh as the last run.\n\nStream processing handles events continuously as they arrive, with latency in milliseconds to seconds, using platforms such as Apache Kafka, Amazon Kinesis, Google Pub/Sub, and processors such as Apache Flink or Spark Structured Streaming. It enables real-time fraud detection, monitoring, alerting, live dashboards, and personalisation, but is more complex to build and operate (ordering, late data, exactly-once semantics, state management). Many architectures combine both.",
    "A card issuer scores each transaction in real time with a streaming pipeline to block fraud, while producing monthly statements with nightly batch jobs.",
    "Choose streaming only when the business truly needs low latency. Batch is simpler and cheaper for everything else."
  ),
  'cda-6': n(
    "Streaming analytics computes results continuously over data in motion. Because streams never end, calculations use windows: tumbling windows (fixed, non-overlapping periods, such as each minute), sliding or hopping windows (overlapping periods), and session windows (activity separated by gaps). Engines also perform stream enrichment (joining events with reference data), pattern detection (complex event processing), and stateful aggregations.\n\nChallenges include late or out-of-order events (handled with event-time processing and watermarks), exactly-once processing guarantees, state checkpointing for recovery, and back-pressure. Tools include Apache Flink, Spark Structured Streaming, Kafka Streams, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, Google Dataflow, and Azure Stream Analytics.",
    "A security platform counts failed logins per user in 5-minute sliding windows and triggers an alert when any user exceeds 20.",
    "Use event time (when it happened), not processing time (when it arrived), for accurate results with delayed data."
  ),
  'cda-7': n(
    "Business intelligence tools, such as Microsoft Power BI, Tableau, Looker, Amazon QuickSight, and Apache Superset, connect to warehouses, lakehouses, and databases and let people explore data through interactive dashboards, reports, and visual analysis without writing code. Semantic layers define shared business metrics (such as 'active customer' and 'net revenue') once so everyone gets consistent numbers.\n\nReal value comes from trusted, governed data and well-designed dashboards aimed at decisions, not from more charts. Good practice includes certified datasets, row-level security so users see only their data, clear metric definitions, performance optimisation through extracts, caching, and aggregates, scheduled report delivery, and self-service within guardrails.",
    "Regional managers open a Power BI dashboard filtered by row-level security to their own stores, showing sales against target and inventory alerts.",
    "Row-level security in BI tools prevents one department or customer from seeing another's data."
  ),
  'cda-8': n(
    "Data mesh is an organisational and architectural approach for large companies where a central data team has become a bottleneck. It rests on four principles: domain ownership (the teams closest to the data, such as orders or marketing, own their analytical data), data as a product (datasets are published with owners, documentation, SLAs, schemas, and quality guarantees), a self-serve data platform (shared infrastructure and tooling so domains can build products easily), and federated computational governance (global standards for security, interoperability, and privacy, enforced automatically).\n\nConsumers discover data products through a catalogue. Data mesh can improve scalability and data quality at large organisations but requires mature teams, platform investment, and cultural change, so it is usually overkill for small teams.",
    "The payments domain team publishes a 'settled_transactions' data product with a documented schema, freshness SLA, and quality tests that finance and risk teams consume.",
    "Federated governance is essential. Without shared standards, a mesh becomes many incompatible data silos."
  ),
  'cda-9': n(
    "Partitioning organises a large dataset into separate folders or segments based on column values, most commonly date (year/month/day), region, or tenant. When a query filters on the partition column, the engine skips all irrelevant partitions (partition pruning) and reads far less data. In pay-per-scan systems such as Athena and BigQuery, that directly cuts cost as well as time.\n\nPartitioning works best combined with columnar file formats (Parquet, ORC), which let engines read only the needed columns, with compression, and with appropriately sized files (avoiding millions of tiny files). Clustering or sorting within partitions and file statistics (min/max per column) enable further skipping. Choose partition keys that match common filters and avoid over-partitioning into too many small partitions.",
    "Partitioning three years of web logs by date means a query for yesterday scans 1 GB instead of 1 TB, and costs about a thousandth as much.",
    "Filter on the partition column in your WHERE clause. Otherwise the engine scans everything anyway."
  ),
  'cda-10': n(
    "Data observability monitors the health of data pipelines and datasets the way application observability monitors services. Key signals are freshness (is the data up to date, and did the pipeline run?), volume (did row counts suddenly drop or spike?), schema (did columns change unexpectedly?), quality or distribution (null rates, duplicates, out-of-range values, referential integrity), and lineage (which sources and transformations produced each table, and what depends on it downstream).\n\nTools include dbt tests, Great Expectations and Soda for assertions, and data observability platforms such as Monte Carlo that detect anomalies automatically. Lineage speeds root cause analysis and impact assessment. Catching bad data before it reaches dashboards and ML models protects decisions and trust.",
    "A volume check alerts that yesterday's orders table has 40% fewer rows than normal; lineage traces it to a failed API extraction before executives see a false sales drop.",
    "Treat data tests like unit tests. Run them in the pipeline and block bad data from being published."
  ),

  // ---------- Cloud Monitoring & Observability ----------
  'cmo-1': n(
    "Monitoring collects predefined metrics and checks them against known thresholds to answer questions you already know to ask: is CPU above 90%? Is the error rate above 1%? Is the site up? It is essential for detecting known failure modes and triggering alerts.\n\nObservability is the ability to understand a system's internal state from its external outputs well enough to answer new questions you did not anticipate, without deploying new code. It relies on rich, high-cardinality telemetry, meaning metrics, logs, and traces with detailed attributes (customer ID, version, region), so engineers can slice and correlate data to explain novel failures in complex distributed systems. In short, monitoring tells you that something is wrong, and observability helps you discover why.",
    "Monitoring alerts that p99 latency jumped; observability lets the engineer find that only users of app version 4.2 in one region, calling one endpoint, are affected.",
    "You need both: monitoring for known problems and fast alerting, observability for investigating the unknown."
  ),
  'cmo-2': n(
    "Metrics are numeric measurements recorded over time as time series: request rate, error count, latency percentiles, CPU and memory utilisation, queue depth, disk I/O. They are compact and cheap to store and query, which makes them ideal for dashboards, trend analysis, capacity planning, and alerting.\n\nTwo frameworks guide what to measure. The RED method for request-driven services tracks Rate (requests per second), Errors (failed requests), and Duration (latency distribution). The USE method for resources tracks Utilisation, Saturation (queued work), and Errors. Use percentiles (p50, p95, p99) rather than averages for latency, because averages hide slow outliers. Watch cardinality: labels with unbounded values, such as user IDs, create huge numbers of time series and costs.",
    "A dashboard shows request rate, 5xx error percentage, and p95 latency for each API; p95 rising from 200 ms to 2 s triggers investigation before users complain.",
    "Averages lie. Alert on percentiles and error rates tied to user experience."
  ),
  'cmo-3': n(
    "Logs are timestamped records of discrete events: HTTP requests, authentication attempts, errors with stack traces, configuration changes, and deployments. They provide the detailed context needed to explain what happened. Structured logging writes each entry as machine-parseable data (typically JSON) with consistent fields such as timestamp, level, service, environment, trace_id, and user_id, making logs searchable and aggregatable.\n\nCentralise logs from all hosts, containers, and services into one platform (CloudWatch Logs, Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, Splunk, Loki, Datadog) using agents or collectors; otherwise debugging means SSHing into dozens of machines, and logs vanish when containers die. Manage volume with log levels, sampling, and retention tiers; never log secrets, passwords, or unnecessary personal data; and protect security logs from tampering.",
    "Searching all services for trace_id=abc123 instantly shows the full sequence of log lines for one failed customer checkout across five microservices.",
    "Redact secrets and PII before logs leave the application. Leaked tokens in logs are a common breach source."
  ),
  'cmo-5': n(
    "Alerting notifies people when a system needs attention. Good alerts are actionable (someone must do something now), urgent, tied to user impact rather than internal symptoms, few in number, and linked to a runbook. Critical alerts page the on-call engineer through tools such as PagerDuty or Opsgenie; lower-severity issues create tickets or chat messages to handle during working hours.\n\nToo many noisy, non-actionable alerts cause alert fatigue, and real incidents get ignored. Improve signal by alerting on SLO burn rate and error rates rather than raw CPU, using durations and multiple windows to avoid flapping, grouping related alerts, suppressing alerts during maintenance, and reviewing every page afterwards: was it useful, and should it be tuned, automated, or deleted?",
    "Instead of paging on 'CPU > 80%', the team pages on 'checkout error budget burning 10x faster than sustainable', which cuts pages by 70% while catching real outages sooner.",
    "Every page should answer: what is broken, how badly are users affected, and where is the runbook?"
  ),
  'cmo-6': n(
    "A Service Level Indicator is a quantitative measure of service behaviour from the user's point of view, such as the proportion of successful requests or the proportion of requests served faster than 300 ms. A Service Level Objective is an internal target for an SLI over a time window, such as 99.9% of requests succeeding over 30 days. A Service Level Agreement is an external contract with customers that specifies consequences, typically service credits, if targets are missed; SLAs are usually looser than internal SLOs.\n\nThe error budget is the allowed unreliability implied by the SLO: 100% − 99.9% = 0.1%, about 43 minutes of downtime or 1 in 1,000 failed requests per month. Teams spend it on releases and experiments; when it runs low, they slow changes and prioritise reliability. This makes reliability versus velocity a data-driven decision.",
    "An API with a 99.95% monthly SLO has an error budget of about 21.6 minutes; after an incident uses 18 minutes, risky launches are postponed to next month.",
    "Set SLOs from user expectations. 100% is the wrong target for almost everything."
  ),
  'cmo-7': n(
    "Synthetic monitoring runs scripted, artificial transactions against your service on a schedule from multiple locations, whether or not real users are active. Checks range from simple HTTP and TCP pings, DNS resolution, and TLS certificate validity and expiry, to multi-step browser scripts that log in, search, and complete a checkout. Tools include CloudWatch Synthetics, Datadog Synthetics, Checkly, and Pingdom.\n\nSynthetics catch outages, broken user journeys, regional problems, and expiring certificates before users report them, and they give consistent baselines. Canary releases are a different but related idea: deploying a new version to a small slice of real traffic first, and comparing its error and latency metrics with the stable version before rolling out further, with automatic rollback if it degrades.",
    "A synthetic browser test detects at 3 a.m. that the login page fails in Europe after a CDN change, and the on-call engineer fixes it before business hours.",
    "Monitor certificate expiry with synthetics. Expired certificates cause surprisingly many major outages."
  ),
  'cmo-8': n(
    "A runbook documents the response to a specific alert or known issue: what it means, how to assess impact, which dashboards, logs, and queries to check, common causes, step-by-step mitigation (including exact commands and rollback procedures), escalation contacts, and how to verify recovery. It lets any on-call engineer respond quickly and consistently, even at 3 a.m. or on an unfamiliar service.\n\nIncident response organises the human side: detect (an alert or report), triage (severity and impact), mobilise (an incident commander, communications lead, and subject-matter experts), mitigate (restore service first, often by rollback or failover), resolve, and learn through a blameless postmortem with tracked action items. Status pages and regular stakeholder updates keep everyone informed.",
    "A database-replication-lag alert links to a runbook that checks the replica's metrics, identifies a long-running query, and shows the safe command to terminate it.",
    "Update the runbook after every incident, while the details are fresh."
  ),
  'cmo-9': n(
    "Each major cloud has a native observability stack. AWS CloudWatch provides metrics, logs (Logs Insights queries), alarms, dashboards, synthetics (canaries), and application signals, with X-Ray for tracing and CloudTrail for API audit. Azure Monitor includes metrics, Log Analytics (KQL queries), Application Insights for APM, alerts, and workbooks. Google Cloud Observability (formerly Stackdriver) provides Cloud Monitoring, Logging, Trace, and Profiler.\n\nNative tools integrate automatically with provider services and IAM, need little setup, and are often enough for cloud-native workloads. Limitations include cross-cloud and hybrid visibility, query and visualisation depth compared with specialist tools, and costs at high log volumes. Many organisations combine native services with OpenTelemetry-based instrumentation and third-party platforms (Datadog, Grafana, New Relic, Splunk) for a unified multi-cloud view.",
    "A team sets CloudWatch alarms on ALB 5xx rates and Lambda errors, and uses Logs Insights to query error patterns across all functions in seconds.",
    "Control log ingestion and retention settings. Default 'never expire' log retention quietly grows costs."
  ),
  'cmo-10': n(
    "OpenTelemetry (OTel) is a vendor-neutral, open-source standard (a CNCF project) for generating and collecting telemetry: traces, metrics, and logs. It provides language SDKs and APIs, automatic instrumentation for popular frameworks and libraries (HTTP servers, database clients, messaging), semantic conventions for consistent attribute names, and the OTLP protocol for sending data.\n\nThe OpenTelemetry Collector receives, processes (batching, filtering, sampling, redacting sensitive data), and exports telemetry to any compatible backend: Prometheus, Jaeger, Tempo, Elasticsearch, Datadog, Honeycomb, New Relic, or cloud-native services. Instrument once and switch or combine backends without re-instrumenting code, which avoids vendor lock-in. It has become the industry standard supported by virtually all observability vendors.",
    "A company instruments all services with OpenTelemetry and later moves from one APM vendor to another by changing the Collector's exporter configuration, not application code.",
    "Use the Collector to scrub sensitive attributes and sample centrally, rather than configuring every service individually."
  ),

  // ---------- Cloud Governance & Compliance ----------
  'cgc-1': n(
    "Cloud governance is the framework of policies, processes, roles, and automated controls that keeps cloud usage secure, compliant, cost-effective, and aligned with business goals as it scales across many accounts, regions, and teams. It covers identity and access, security baselines, network architecture, data protection and residency, cost management, resource tagging, and approved services.\n\nModern governance is automated rather than based on manual approvals: landing zones (AWS Control Tower, Azure Landing Zones) create standardised, pre-secured accounts; organisation-level guardrails (service control policies, Azure Policy) prevent forbidden actions; detective controls (AWS Config, CSPM) flag drift; and dashboards give visibility. Good governance enables teams to move fast safely instead of blocking them. Without it, cloud sprawl produces uncontrolled costs, security gaps, and compliance failures.",
    "A new team requests an AWS account and receives one in minutes through Control Tower, already configured with logging, guardrails blocking public S3, and budget alerts.",
    "Prefer preventive guardrails (block the action) over detective ones (alert afterwards) for high-risk misconfigurations."
  ),
  'cgc-2': n(
    "Policy as code expresses governance and security rules in machine-readable files that are version-controlled, peer-reviewed, tested, and evaluated automatically, instead of written checklists and manual reviews. Policies can run at several points: in CI/CD against infrastructure-as-code plans before deployment (Checkov, tfsec, OPA/Conftest, Terraform Sentinel), at admission time in Kubernetes (OPA Gatekeeper, Kyverno), and continuously at runtime against deployed resources (AWS Config rules, Azure Policy, GCP Organization Policy).\n\nExamples include requiring encryption on all storage, blocking public buckets, enforcing mandatory tags, restricting regions, and forbidding privileged containers. Benefits are consistency, fast feedback to developers, an audit trail of policy changes, and scalability across thousands of resources. Exceptions should be explicit, documented, and time-limited.",
    "A pull request adding an unencrypted RDS database fails the pipeline because a Checkov policy requires storage encryption; the developer fixes it before merging.",
    "Shift left: catching violations in CI is far cheaper than finding them in production."
  ),
  'cgc-3': n(
    "Cloud Security Posture Management tools continuously scan cloud accounts and services for misconfigurations and compliance violations, such as publicly accessible storage, unencrypted volumes and databases, overly permissive IAM policies, security groups open to the internet, disabled logging, and missing MFA on privileged accounts. They compare configurations with benchmarks such as the CIS Benchmarks, NIST, PCI DSS, and ISO 27001, and prioritise findings by risk.\n\nExamples include AWS Security Hub, Microsoft Defender for Cloud, Google Security Command Center, and third-party platforms (Wiz, Prisma Cloud, Orca), which often combine CSPM with workload protection and identity analysis as part of a CNAPP. Value comes from acting on findings: assigning owners, automating remediation for common issues, and tracking the posture score over time.",
    "CSPM flags a newly created S3 bucket that allows public read and an IAM user with no MFA; automated remediation blocks public access within minutes.",
    "Misconfiguration, not zero-day exploits, causes most cloud breaches. CSPM targets exactly that risk."
  ),
  'cgc-4': n(
    "SOC 2 is an attestation framework from the AICPA in which an independent auditor reports on a service organisation's controls against the Trust Services Criteria: security (required), availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy. A Type I report assesses control design at a point in time; a Type II report tests operating effectiveness over a period, usually 6–12 months, which is what customers typically ask SaaS vendors for.\n\nISO/IEC 27001 is an international, certifiable standard for an information security management system (ISMS): a risk-based management process with policies, a risk assessment, a Statement of Applicability selecting controls from Annex A, internal audits, and continual improvement, verified by accredited certification bodies. Both require evidence and continuous operation, not one-time documents. Cloud providers publish their reports, and customers inherit infrastructure controls but must implement their own.",
    "An enterprise customer requires a SaaS vendor's SOC 2 Type II report and ISO 27001 certificate before signing, and reviews the vendor's control exceptions.",
    "SOC 2 = an auditor's attestation report (Type I or II). ISO 27001 = certification of a management system."
  ),
  'cgc-5': n(
    "PCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard, currently version 4.0) applies to any organisation that stores, processes, or transmits cardholder data. Its twelve requirements cover network security controls, secure configurations, protecting stored account data (encryption, masking, and minimal retention), encrypting transmission, anti-malware, secure development, restricting access by need to know, strong authentication with MFA, physical security, logging and monitoring, regular testing (scans and penetration tests), and security policies.\n\nHIPAA protects US protected health information (PHI) through the Privacy Rule, Security Rule (administrative, physical, and technical safeguards), and Breach Notification Rule; cloud providers handling PHI must sign a Business Associate Agreement (BAA). Reducing scope (for example tokenising card data or using a payment provider's hosted checkout) is often the most effective compliance strategy.",
    "An online shop uses a payment provider's hosted checkout page, so card numbers never touch its servers, dramatically shrinking its PCI DSS scope.",
    "Scope reduction beats scope compliance. Keep regulated data out of systems that do not need it."
  ),
  'cgc-6': n(
    "Cost governance applies policies and automation so cloud spending stays visible, attributable, and controlled. Foundations include mandatory tagging (owner, cost centre, environment, application) enforced by policy, account structure aligned to teams or products, and cost allocation reports and dashboards. Budgets with alerts at thresholds (for example 50%, 80%, and 100% of forecast) warn owners before overruns, and cost anomaly detection flags unexpected spikes, often caused by misconfigurations, runaway autoscaling, forgotten resources, or compromised accounts used for crypto-mining.\n\nPreventive guardrails can restrict expensive instance types or regions, require approval for large purchases, and automatically shut down idle non-production resources. FinOps provides the culture and collaboration; governance provides the automated enforcement.",
    "An anomaly alert shows GPU spend jumping from $50 to $4,000 a day; investigation finds leaked credentials being used for crypto-mining, and the keys are revoked.",
    "Cost anomalies can be security incidents. Route anomaly alerts to security as well as finance."
  ),
  'cgc-7': n(
    "Audit logs record who did what, when, from where, and with what result across cloud control planes and data access: AWS CloudTrail (API calls), Azure Activity Log and resource logs, Google Cloud Audit Logs, plus service-specific data-access logs, VPC flow logs, and identity provider sign-in logs. They are essential for compliance evidence, security monitoring, and incident investigation.\n\nProtect their integrity and availability: enable logging organisation-wide in every region, deliver logs to a separate, locked-down logging or security account with write-once (object lock or immutable) storage and log-file integrity validation, restrict access tightly, set retention to meet regulatory and investigative needs (often a year or more), and stream them to a SIEM for real-time detection. Attackers routinely try to disable or delete logs to cover their tracks.",
    "During an investigation, CloudTrail logs in the isolated security account show exactly which access key created new IAM users at 02:14 and from which IP.",
    "Alert immediately on attempts to stop or delete audit logging (for example CloudTrail StopLogging)."
  ),
  'cgc-8': n(
    "Data residency is the requirement that data be stored, and sometimes processed, in a particular geographic location. Data sovereignty goes further: data is subject to the laws and jurisdiction of the country where it resides, which can include government access rights. Regulations and contracts (GDPR transfer rules, national data localisation laws, public-sector requirements) often dictate where personal, financial, or government data may live.\n\nCloud controls include choosing specific regions, restricting allowed regions with organisation policies, disabling cross-region replication and global services for regulated data, customer-managed keys (optionally held outside the provider), and sovereign or dedicated cloud offerings. Compliance requires knowing your data flows, including backups, logs, analytics copies, support access, and third-party SaaS processors, and documenting them.",
    "A German public-sector client requires all data in Germany; the architecture uses the Frankfurt region only, with a policy blocking resource creation elsewhere.",
    "Backups, logs, and disaster-recovery copies count too. They often silently replicate data outside the required region."
  ),
  'cgc-9': n(
    "An IAM permission boundary is an advanced AWS feature that sets the maximum permissions an IAM user or role can ever have. Effective permissions are the intersection of the identity's policies and its boundary, so even if someone attaches AdministratorAccess, the identity can do only what the boundary also allows. (Service control policies provide a similar cap at the account or organisation level, and Azure uses scoped roles and management groups for comparable control.)\n\nBoundaries enable safe delegation: a platform team can let application teams create their own IAM roles for their services, but require that every role they create carries a boundary, so teams cannot create roles more powerful than themselves or escalate privileges. This combines developer autonomy with centrally controlled limits.",
    "Developers may create Lambda execution roles, but only with the 'app-boundary' policy attached, which forbids IAM changes and access outside their project's resources.",
    "Effective permissions = identity policy ∩ permission boundary ∩ SCPs, with any explicit Deny winning."
  ),
  'cgc-10': n(
    "Joiner-Mover-Leaver (JML) processes manage access across an identity's lifecycle. Joiners receive baseline access for their role automatically on day one. Movers who change roles gain the new role's access and, critically, lose the old role's; missing this step causes privilege creep. Leavers have all access revoked promptly (ideally automatically, triggered by HR systems), including accounts, tokens, keys, and shared credentials.\n\nPeriodic access reviews (user access recertification) have managers or resource owners confirm that each person still needs each entitlement, removing what is unnecessary; privileged and sensitive access is reviewed more often. Identity governance tools (such as SailPoint, Entra ID Governance, and Okta Identity Governance) automate provisioning through SCIM, reviews, and reporting. Stale and orphaned accounts are a frequent breach cause and a common audit finding.",
    "When an employee transfers from finance to marketing, automation removes their access to the accounting system the same day, before their new marketing access is granted.",
    "Revoke leavers' access on or before their last day, including API keys and personal access tokens, not just their main login."
  ),

  // ---------- Monitoring & Observability (advanced) ----------
  'moa-1': n(
    "OpenTelemetry's architecture has three parts. First, instrumentation in each application, either automatic (agents and libraries that instrument frameworks without code changes) or manual (the API used to add custom spans, metrics, and business attributes). Second, the OpenTelemetry Collector, deployed as an agent on each host or node and/or as a central gateway, which receives telemetry over OTLP and other protocols, processes it (batching, memory limiting, attribute enrichment such as Kubernetes metadata, redaction, and tail-based sampling), and exports it. Third, one or more backends that store and analyse the data.\n\nThis decoupling means you can change or combine backends by reconfiguring exporters, add enrichment centrally, and control costs through sampling in one place. Semantic conventions keep attribute names consistent across languages and teams.",
    "A Collector gateway enriches all spans with Kubernetes pod and namespace labels, drops health-check traces, keeps 100% of error traces, and sends the data to two backends during a vendor migration.",
    "Deploy Collectors close to workloads (agent mode) plus a gateway tier, so application SDKs stay simple and configuration lives centrally."
  ),
  'moa-2': n(
    "Service maps (dependency or topology maps) are generated automatically from tracing data: every span that calls another service defines an edge, building a live graph of which services call which, with request rates, error rates, and latency on each connection. Because they are derived from real traffic, they reveal dependencies nobody documented, unexpected callers, circular dependencies, and changes after deployments.\n\nDuring incidents, service maps show blast radius at a glance: which upstream services are affected by a failing dependency, and where errors originate versus where they surface. They also support architecture reviews, capacity planning, and security reviews by showing actual data flows.",
    "During an outage, the service map shows the failing recommendation service is called synchronously by checkout, explaining why a 'non-critical' service broke purchases.",
    "Critical user paths should not depend synchronously on non-critical services. Service maps expose those hidden couplings."
  ),
  'moa-3': n(
    "Google's SRE book defines four golden signals for monitoring user-facing systems. Latency is the time taken to serve requests, measured as a distribution (p50, p95, p99), with successful and failed requests tracked separately, because fast errors can make averages look good. Traffic is the demand on the system, such as requests per second, transactions, or concurrent sessions.\n\nErrors are the rate of failing requests, whether explicit (HTTP 5xx), implicit (wrong content returned with a 200), or policy-based (responses slower than the SLO). Saturation is how 'full' the service is: utilisation of its most constrained resource (CPU, memory, disk I/O, connection or thread pools, queue depth), which often predicts problems before errors appear. Dashboards and alerts built around these four cover most user-impacting failures.",
    "Queue depth (saturation) climbs for 20 minutes before latency and errors spike, so alerting on saturation gives the team time to scale out first.",
    "Track latency of successful and failed requests separately. Quick failures can mask a real outage in averages."
  ),
  'moa-4': n(
    "Application Performance Monitoring (APM) instruments application code to measure transactions end to end: response times per endpoint, slow database queries, external API calls, errors with stack traces, and throughput, often linking directly to distributed traces. It answers 'which part of this request is slow?' at the code level.\n\nContinuous profiling samples CPU usage, memory allocations, locks, and I/O across production processes at low overhead, aggregating them into flame graphs that show which functions consume resources over time. It catches issues APM can miss: memory leaks, inefficient serialisation, regex backtracking, excessive garbage collection, and gradual performance regressions between releases. Tools include Pyroscope and Grafana, Datadog, Parca, and cloud profilers. Use APM for latency paths and profiling for resource hotspots.",
    "Continuous profiling reveals that JSON serialisation of a large response object uses 40% of CPU after a release; switching the serialiser halves the server count needed.",
    "Compare profiles before and after each release to catch performance regressions early."
  ),
  'moa-5': n(
    "Log aggregation collects logs from every host, container, function, and service into a central platform where they can be searched, correlated, visualised, and alerted on. Collectors and shippers (Fluent Bit, Fluentd, Vector, the OpenTelemetry Collector, and cloud agents) read logs, parse and enrich them (adding host, pod, and environment metadata), filter and redact sensitive fields, and send them to backends such as Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, Loki, Splunk, or cloud log services.\n\nFor useful analysis, use consistent structured fields (timestamp, service, severity, trace_id, user or tenant ID), index what you search frequently, build saved queries and dashboards, and feed security detections and anomaly alerts. Plan retention by value: short hot retention for debugging, longer cold or archive retention for compliance and forensics, and tight access controls.",
    "A security analyst searches a year of centralised authentication logs to find every login by a compromised account across all systems in under a minute.",
    "Centralise logs off the host quickly. Attackers delete local logs, and containers take theirs with them when they die."
  ),
  'moa-6': n(
    "Burn rate measures how fast an SLO's error budget is being consumed relative to the rate that would use it up exactly at the end of the SLO window. A burn rate of 1 means you will use the whole budget in 30 days; a burn rate of 14.4 sustained for an hour consumes about 2% of a 30-day budget, a typical threshold for paging.\n\nMulti-window, multi-burn-rate alerting (from the Google SRE Workbook) pairs a long window with a short one, for example page if the 1-hour and 5-minute burn rates both exceed 14.4, and open a ticket if the 3-day and 6-hour rates both exceed 1. The long window proves the problem is significant; the short window confirms it is still happening, so alerts reset quickly after recovery. This yields fewer false pages, fast detection of severe outages, and alerts aligned with business tolerance.",
    "A brief 2-minute error spike no longer pages anyone, but a sustained 5% error rate pages within minutes because it would exhaust the monthly budget in hours.",
    "Page on fast burn, ticket on slow burn. Both are tied to the SLO, not to arbitrary thresholds."
  ),
  'moa-7': n(
    "Runbook automation turns repetitive, well-understood response steps into code triggered by alerts or run with one click: gathering diagnostics (logs, thread dumps, recent deploys, and metrics snapshots attached to the incident), restarting a known-safe stuck process, clearing a cache, scaling out, failing over, rotating a leaked credential, or rolling back the last deployment. Tools include AWS Systems Manager Automation, Rundeck, StackStorm, PagerDuty Runbook Automation, and SOAR platforms for security.\n\nAutomation reduces mean time to recovery and removes error-prone manual steps under pressure. Keep humans in the loop for novel or high-risk situations: automate what is safe, idempotent, and well tested, add guardrails and rate limits, log every automated action, and review automations after incidents.",
    "A disk-full alert automatically runs a job that compresses old logs and, if space is still low, expands the volume, notifying the on-call engineer only if both steps fail.",
    "Only automate actions that are safe to run repeatedly (idempotent), with clear limits on how often they fire."
  ),
  'moa-8': n(
    "Chaos engineering deliberately injects failures, such as terminating instances or pods, adding network latency or packet loss, exhausting CPU or memory, failing a dependency, or blocking a zone, to test whether systems and teams handle them as expected. It is run as controlled experiments: define the steady state, hypothesise what will happen, limit the blast radius, run the experiment, and observe. Tools include AWS Fault Injection Service, Gremlin, Chaos Mesh, and LitmusChaos; Netflix's Chaos Monkey pioneered the approach.\n\nFault injection also validates observability: if the dashboards, alerts, and runbooks do not detect an injected failure, they will not detect a real one. Game days bring teams together to practise realistic scenarios, test on-call readiness, and find gaps safely.",
    "A game day that blocks one AZ reveals that a critical cache runs in a single AZ; the team fixes it before a real outage exposes the weakness.",
    "Start small in non-production, with an abort button, then graduate to carefully scoped production experiments."
  ),
  'moa-9': n(
    "Telemetry costs grow with volume (log bytes ingested, spans stored), metric cardinality (unique label combinations), and retention. Unmanaged, observability can become one of the largest items on the infrastructure bill. Cost-aware observability keeps the diagnostic value while controlling spend.\n\nTechniques include: sampling traces (head-based sampling for routine traffic, and tail-based sampling that keeps all errors and slow requests); dropping or aggregating noisy, low-value logs (debug logs, health checks) at the collector; converting high-volume logs into metrics; limiting high-cardinality labels to those with clear diagnostic value; tiered retention (hot for recent debugging, cheaper warm storage for weeks, archive for compliance); and attributing telemetry costs to the teams that generate them.",
    "Tail-based sampling keeps 100% of error traces but only 5% of successful ones, cutting tracing costs by 80% while preserving everything needed to debug failures.",
    "Never put unbounded values such as user IDs or request IDs in metric labels. Put them in logs or traces instead."
  ),
  'moa-10': n(
    "Effective incident diagnosis correlates signals across telemetry types and change events. Start from the symptom (an SLO or golden-signal alert), narrow the scope with metrics (which service, region, version, or customer segment?), use traces to find the failing or slow component, read that component's logs for the specific error, and check change events: deployments, feature flag flips, configuration changes, infrastructure events, and dependency incidents.\n\nShared identifiers make this fast: trace IDs in logs, request IDs, deployment version labels on metrics, and deployment markers on dashboards. Afterwards, document the causal chain in the postmortem with concrete evidence (graphs, log lines, and trace links) rather than guesses, and distinguish the root cause from contributing factors and triggers.",
    "A latency spike lines up exactly with a deployment marker; traces point to a new database query, and logs show it running without an index.",
    "Always overlay deployment and config-change markers on dashboards. Changes are the most common incident trigger."
  ),
  'mo-1': n(
    "Prometheus is the leading open-source metrics system for cloud-native environments (a CNCF graduated project). It uses a pull model: the Prometheus server scrapes HTTP /metrics endpoints on targets at regular intervals, discovering targets dynamically (for example via Kubernetes service discovery), and stores the samples in a time-series database. Exporters expose metrics for systems that do not do so natively (node_exporter for hosts, database exporters, and so on).\n\nMetric types are counters (only increase, such as total requests), gauges (go up and down, such as memory in use), histograms (bucketed observations such as request duration, enabling percentiles), and summaries. PromQL queries them: rate() computes per-second rates from counters, increase() gives totals over a window, and histogram_quantile() computes percentiles. Recording rules precompute expensive queries; alerting rules feed Alertmanager for routing, grouping, and silencing.",
    "`histogram_quantile(0.95, sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])) by (le, service))` shows p95 latency per service.",
    "Always apply rate() to counters. Raw counter values only ever grow and are meaningless on their own."
  ),
  'mo-2': n(
    "Structured logging emits each log event as machine-parseable data, usually a JSON object, instead of free-form text. Consistent fields such as timestamp (ISO 8601 in UTC), level, service, environment, version, message, trace_id and span_id, request_id, and relevant business keys (order_id, tenant_id) make logs easy to filter, aggregate, and correlate across services.\n\nMost languages have structured logging libraries (for example Python's structlog, Java's Logback with a JSON encoder, Go's slog, and Node's pino). Ship logs to a centralised store (Loki, Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, Splunk, or cloud log services) with consistent parsing. Redact secrets, tokens, passwords, and unnecessary personal data before shipping, and use log levels thoughtfully so production is not flooded with debug output.",
    "`{\"ts\":\"2026-09-27T10:15:02Z\",\"level\":\"error\",\"service\":\"payments\",\"trace_id\":\"7f3a…\",\"order_id\":\"A1021\",\"msg\":\"card declined by issuer\"}` can be searched by any field.",
    "Agree on field names across teams (or use OpenTelemetry semantic conventions) so cross-service searches just work."
  ),
  'mo-3': n(
    "In microservices, one user request may touch a dozen services, queues, and databases. Distributed tracing reconstructs that journey: the entry point (often the API gateway) starts a trace, and each service records spans for its work, with timing, status, and attributes, passing the trace context to the next service through headers or message metadata (W3C Trace Context).\n\nThe resulting trace, displayed as a timeline (waterfall), shows the critical path, parallel versus sequential calls, retries, and where errors originate. OpenTelemetry provides the instrumentation; Jaeger, Zipkin, Grafana Tempo, AWS X-Ray, and commercial APM tools provide storage and visualisation. Span metrics derived from traces feed service maps and RED dashboards.",
    "A trace shows an order request calling inventory, pricing, and shipping sequentially; making the three calls in parallel cuts latency from 900 ms to 350 ms.",
    "Propagate trace context through message queues and async jobs too, or traces break at every asynchronous hop."
  ),
  'mo-5': n(
    "SLO-based alerting ties alerts to user-visible reliability rather than to internal resource thresholds. Define SLIs that reflect the user experience (availability as successful requests over total, latency as the proportion of requests under a threshold), set SLOs (for example 99.9% over 28 days), and derive the error budget.\n\nAlert on how fast the error budget is burning, not on each individual missed request or CPU spike: multi-window, multi-burn-rate alerts page for fast, significant burns and create tickets for slow burns. This catches real outages quickly, ignores brief blips, and reduces pager noise. Revisit SLOs periodically as user expectations and system capabilities change, and use remaining error budget to guide release decisions.",
    "A team replaces 40 threshold alerts with 6 burn-rate alerts on 3 SLOs; pages drop by 80% while every real customer-impacting incident is still caught.",
    "If an alert does not correspond to user pain or imminent risk, it should not page a human."
  ),
  'mo-6': n(
    "Dashboards turn telemetry into understanding, but only if they are designed for a purpose and an audience. Service dashboards typically follow the RED method (Rate, Errors, Duration) and the golden signals, plus SLO status and error budget remaining, saturation of key resources, dependency health, and deployment markers. Executive and business dashboards show SLO compliance, availability trends, and key business metrics.\n\nGood practice: a clear hierarchy (overview first, with drill-down links), consistent time ranges and units, percentiles rather than averages, annotations for deployments and incidents, and dashboards defined as code (for example Grafana JSON or Terraform) so they are versioned and reviewed. Avoid dashboard sprawl, meaning hundreds of unmaintained dashboards nobody trusts.",
    "The on-call overview dashboard shows every service's SLO status in green, amber, or red; clicking a red one opens that service's RED dashboard with recent deploys marked.",
    "If nobody has opened a dashboard in 90 days, archive it. Curated beats comprehensive."
  ),
  'mo-8': n(
    "Synthetic monitoring actively probes services from the outside on a schedule, simulating users: uptime checks for HTTP, TCP, ICMP, and DNS; API checks that validate response content and latency; SSL certificate checks; and scripted browser journeys (using tools such as Playwright) that log in, search, add to cart, and check out. Probes run from multiple geographic locations and private locations inside your network.\n\nBecause synthetics run even when there is no real traffic, such as at night or for new features, they detect outages and regressions before users report them and give a consistent performance baseline. Combine them with passive, real-user telemetry (RUM, server metrics, and traces) for full coverage from outside and inside. Alert on consecutive failures from multiple locations to avoid false alarms from a single flaky probe.",
    "A scripted checkout journey runs every 5 minutes from four continents; after a payment-provider change, it fails in Asia only, pinpointing a regional routing problem.",
    "Use dedicated test accounts and data for synthetic transactions, and exclude them from business analytics."
  ),
  'mo-9': n(
    "Reliable incident response depends on well-designed on-call practices. Rotations should be sustainable (reasonable frequency, follow-the-sun where possible, and compensation or time off), with clear escalation policies and handoff notes between shifts. Alerts route by severity: pages for urgent, user-impacting issues, and tickets for everything else. Each page links to a runbook.\n\nDuring significant incidents, defined roles keep response organised: an incident commander coordinating, a communications lead updating stakeholders and status pages, and responders investigating and mitigating. Track metrics such as MTTA (mean time to acknowledge), MTTR (mean time to recover), and pages per shift. After each incident, run a blameless review and tune or remove alerts that were noisy or unhelpful.",
    "A team measures that half its night pages were self-resolving blips; tuning those alerts lets on-call engineers sleep, and response times to real incidents improve.",
    "Pager fatigue is a reliability risk. Burnt-out responders make slower, worse decisions."
  ),
  'monitoring-observability-advanced-c22': n(
    "Alert quality determines whether on-call engineers trust and act on alerts. A high-quality alert requires human action now, reflects real or imminent user impact, is specific about what is wrong, and links to a runbook and relevant dashboards. Low-quality alerts, such as flapping thresholds, duplicate notifications, informational messages, or problems that fix themselves, create noise that trains people to ignore pages.\n\nImprove quality by alerting on symptoms (SLO burn, error rate) rather than every possible cause, requiring conditions to persist before firing, using multi-window checks, grouping related alerts, routing by severity, suppressing during planned maintenance and temporarily after deployments, and reviewing alert statistics regularly. Delete or downgrade any alert that has not led to meaningful action.",
    "An alert review finds a disk alert that fired 200 times last month with no action taken; it is changed to a ticket at a higher threshold, freeing on-call attention.",
    "Measure alert precision: of the pages sent, how many required action? Aim for nearly all of them."
  ),
};
